# Documentation & CTO Handoff Standards

Every code change must leave the codebase ready for a senior engineer to pick up cold.
The target audience is the incoming CTO — assume they have zero project context.

## After Every Feature or Phase

- Update `README.md` with any new setup steps, environment variables, or commands
- Update relevant `docs/*.md` files if the data model, lifecycle, roles, or architecture changed
- If a new environment variable is introduced, add it to `.env.example` with a comment explaining its purpose
- If a new database migration is created, ensure `docs/04-data-model.md` reflects the new/changed entities
- If a new external integration point is added (API, webhook, cron), document it in `docs/10-integrations.md`

## External Integration Points

- Every inbound webhook / API route must have a JSDoc block explaining the expected caller, auth mechanism, and payload shape
- Every outbound integration (Pipedrive, Resend, Bright.Blue Cloud) must have a clearly-labelled stub with `// INTEGRATION: <service>` and a link to the relevant handler
- Stubs for unconnected integrations must throw a descriptive error or return a typed placeholder — never silently no-op without logging
- Document all integration contracts in `docs/10-integrations.md` so the CTO can wire real endpoints without reading implementation code

## File-Level Documentation

- Every new file must have a one-line JSDoc comment at the top explaining what it does
- Every exported function must have a brief JSDoc explaining its purpose and return value
- Complex business logic (pricing, tier lookups, template expansion, telemetry aggregation) must have inline comments explaining the "why," not the "what"

## Changelog

- Maintain a `CHANGELOG.md` at the project root
- Add an entry for each phase or significant feature with date, summary, and files changed
- Format: `## [Phase X] - YYYY-MM-DD` with bullet points
